TITLE='Authentic title "Why $300,000 Net Worth Feels Like Nothing in 2026" uses the channel\'s "why X feels like nothing" threshold-disappointment frame with a year stamp. The script explains perception gaps (inflation, home equity, retirement income, shocks, comparison). TYPICAL but just above the WEAK threshold (~0.51x) against peers including the 294K-view "Why Everything Changes After Paying Off Your House" and its NICK_203 sibling (TYPICAL). Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White field split by a vertical line. Left "$300K BEFORE": smiling cartoon man in a black suit with a gold watch holding a fat wallet of cash. Right "$300K NOW": the same man disheveled and teary, sitting in a pile of cash while holding a near-empty red wallet. The image conveys lost purchasing power, matching the inflation section. At 320x180 both headlines and expressions remain legible.'
SCRIPT='A perception essay: two reactions to the same $300,000, Marcus and Diane (43, Columbus, $115K), wealth distribution and Musk comparison, Schwab wealth thresholds, inflation erosion, category inflation, home equity vs liquidity, 4% rule and Social Security, Vanguard balances, compounding and critical mass, life shocks (emergency fund, college, long-term care), sandwich generation, sequence risk (2007-09, 2022), money dysmorphia and social comparison, and a reassurance close. Verified: Schwab 2025 $2.3M/$839K; many compounding and sum calculations reproduce. Wrong or inconsistent: 2022 loss ($61,500 at the stated 20.5%, not $76,200); Musk $250B vs "$800 billion in a year"; retirement gap ignores the second spouse\'s Social Security; compounding applied to the full $300K after excluding home equity; premiums +54% not +49%. The stored ending is complete.'
